<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haskalah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jewish Enlightenment</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haskalah, often called the Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, was an intellectual movement among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> that arose in the 1770s as its followers, the Maskilim, engaged with the ideas of the Age of Enlightenment. Its first centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> Mendelssohn; it revived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> as a literary language for secular purposes and pressed for educational and cultural reform and integration into the surrounding societies, clashing with both the rabbinic elite and radical assimilationists. Its last stage ended late in the 19th century, after the 1881 pogroms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> turned many Maskilim towards proto-Zionist groups such as Hibbat Zion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haskalah.jpg" alt="Haskalah" width="330" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ביקורת · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haskalah_Colage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Haskalah</b>, the Jewish Enlightenment, encouraged secular learning.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th and 19th centuries, writers of the <b>Jewish Enlightenment</b> used Hebrew for modern literature and journalism, and the Zionist movement made its revival as a spoken language a national cause.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Others have studied the Catholic and <b>Jewish Enlightenments</b>, the movement’s reach into the Americas and the wider world, and its entanglement with slavery and empire.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
